WEBVTT

00:22:00.004 --> 00:22:03.429
so that their office
would have a little bit more privacy.

00:22:03.453 --> 00:22:05.448
Like, if people want privacy, no problem,

00:22:05.472 --> 00:22:06.917
we just put up the vinyl,

00:22:06.941 --> 00:22:09.012
and so you would just see
different things,

00:22:09.036 --> 00:22:11.849
where it's like,
"Oh, that's not allowed at all,"

00:22:11.873 --> 00:22:13.549
which was not true.

00:22:13.573 --> 00:22:15.781
That was one that I always found myself

00:22:15.805 --> 00:22:17.202
being pulled into a meeting,

00:22:17.226 --> 00:22:18.924
like, we're not allowed to do that,

00:22:18.948 --> 00:22:21.591
and I'd be like, "You're absolutely
allowed to do that,

00:22:21.615 --> 00:22:23.815
that's what the member wants,
give it to them,

00:22:23.839 --> 00:22:25.004
it's not a big deal."

00:22:25.028 --> 00:22:27.440
AG: This definitely captures
the double-edged sword

00:22:27.464 --> 00:22:29.745
of strong cultures in a compelling way.

00:22:29.769 --> 00:22:32.612
I think about one of the benefits
of strong cultures being